INT. ER TRAUMA BAY - NIGHT

A curtained bay under harsh fluorescent light. Monitors beep in a staggered chorus. The floor is wet where something was mopped and then spilled again.

On the gurney lies a motorcyclist in a ruined leather jacket, jaw clamped around a bite block. Round him, the team works in a tight ring.

DR. FATIMA HADDAD (40s, trauma lead, surgical cap with cartoon owls) calls out numbers in a flat voice.

DR. PIERRE LEFEBVRE-OKORO (30s, resident, shirt soaked to the elbow) holds pressure on the chest.

NURSE TALIA BRODERICK (50s, silver braid, expert hands) pushes a syringe into a line and calls the drug.

RESPIRATORY THERAPIST GUNNAR EKSTROM (20s, headset, calm) squeezes a bag and watches the numbers climb.

TALIA
Two of epi in.

FATIMA
Pressure?

GUNNAR
Seventy over forty.

PIERRE
I have the bleed. I have it.

FATIMA
Hold it. Hold it, Pierre.

PIERRE
Holding.

The monitor tone changes: a faster beep, a ragged line. Fatima lifts her eyes to it. Nobody speaks. Gunnar squeezes the bag. Talia's hand rests on the patient's shoulder, steady as a stone.

GUNNAR
Eighty over fifty. Ninety.

FATIMA
Sinus rhythm. We have a rhythm.

The tone smooths. A long breath moves around the ring like a wave. Pierre's shoulders drop an inch. Talia taps the shoulder twice, lightly, the way you would pat a horse.

The curtain flicks open. A tall man steps through: DR. EMEKA ADEYEMI-STROM (60s, chief of surgery, tie loose, tired). He takes in the monitors, the blood, the faces, and nods once.

EMEKA
OR two is ready. Who's going up?

FATIMA
Pierre will scrub in with you. I'll stay with the chest tube.

EMEKA
Good.

PIERRE
Good?

EMEKA
Good work. All of you.

He doesn't smile. He doesn't have to. The room has just slowed down to a human speed, and the owls on Fatima's cap seem to blink.

TALIA
(quietly)
Gurney. On three.

They count it together and roll him out.

FADE OUT.
